00:00:36,341 --> 00:00:37,340
Marvels.

9
00:00:49,340 --> 00:00:53,600
When we picture ice, it's usually floating in our glass,

10
00:00:53,601 --> 00:00:58,200
threatening us at sea, or glistening after a man's

11
00:00:58,201 --> 00:01:01,200
glistening after a manicure from a Zamboni machine.

12
00:01:04,100 --> 00:01:08,040
But the epic of ice doesn't stop there.

13
00:01:08,140 --> 00:01:15,960
It continues in another realm we seldom consider, the sky.

14
00:01:16,060 --> 00:01:20,460
Our atmosphere plays host to countless ice particles,

15
00:01:20,560 --> 00:01:23,600
hovering within clouds that cover 60% of the Earth's

16
00:01:23,700 --> 00:01:24,400
surface.

17
00:01:24,401 --> 00:01:29,600
surface. In an ongoing natural cycle,

18
00:01:29,601 --> 00:01:33,500
they respond to variations in temperature and ultimately descend

19
00:01:33,501 --> 00:01:40,340
to join us. If they melt, we get rain.

20
00:01:40,341 --> 00:01:44,060
If they don't melt, we get snow. If they melt but then freeze on

21
00:01:44,061 --> 00:01:46,660
contact with the ground, then we get freezing rain, and that's

22
00:01:46,661 --> 00:01:53,100
where the ice storms come from. One of the 20th century's most

23
00:01:53,101 --> 00:01:58,300
severe ice storms struck Canada in January 1998.

24
00:01:58,400 --> 00:02:00,600
The frigid assault persisted for six days.

25
00:02:03,340 --> 00:02:07,140
Ice as thick as four inches coated Ontario, Quebec,

26
00:02:07,240 --> 00:02:10,560
and New Brunswick.

27
00:02:10,660 --> 00:02:13,660
The burden of the ice's weight toppled 130 power

28
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:19,800
transmission towers, 30,000 utility poles,

29
00:02:19,801 --> 00:02:20,800
of trees.

30
00:02:23,700 --> 00:02:26,800
4 million people lost electricity.

31
00:02:26,900 --> 00:02:31,140
600,000 were forced to abandon their homes.

32
00:02:31,240 --> 00:02:37,460
945 were injured, and 28 people perished.

33
00:02:37,560 --> 00:02:42,060
The storm caused more than $5.4 billion in damage

34
00:02:42,160 --> 00:02:45,100
and served as a chilling reminder of ICE's destructive

35
00:02:45,200 --> 00:02:45,900
power.

36
00:02:49,800 --> 00:02:56,800
Another variety of atmospheric ice freezes miles above us

37
00:02:56,900 --> 00:02:59,800
before crashing down on our heads.

38
00:03:04,740 --> 00:03:09,760
Hail is that odd curiosity as fascinating as it is destructive.

39
00:03:09,860 --> 00:03:11,760
There goes a windshield.

40
00:03:11,860 --> 00:03:17,760
Hailstones are particles of ice created in thunderstorms.

41
00:03:17,761 --> 00:03:24,760
thunderstorms. Thunderstorms can occur year-round

42
00:03:24,761 --> 00:03:28,700
across the globe, but in temperate zones like the United

43
00:03:28,701 --> 00:03:34,700
States, they happen most typically in spring and summer.

44
00:03:34,701 --> 00:03:38,120
The cumulus clouds from which thunderstorms grow possess two

45
00:03:38,121 --> 00:03:42,420
features critical to hail formation, subfreezing

46
00:03:42,421 --> 00:03:46,720
temperatures, even on the hottest day below, and updrafts that

47
00:03:46,721 --> 00:03:49,620
that can keep ice particles suspended for hours at a time.

48
00:03:54,960 --> 00:04:00,060
Hailstorms form in storms where that updraft is strong and tilted.

49
00:04:00,061 --> 00:04:03,160
So what happens is the particle that's growing in the storm

50
00:04:03,161 --> 00:04:06,040
is traveling up the updraft, gets to the top of the storm

51
00:04:06,041 --> 00:04:08,240
where it's weak, and then it falls.

52
00:04:08,241 --> 00:04:10,080
And this cloud can be six miles deep.

53
00:04:10,081 --> 00:04:12,880
And so as those particles fall, they collide with other ice

54
00:04:12,881 --> 00:04:14,840
crystals, and they collect them.

55
00:04:14,841 --> 00:04:16,840
And then when they get near the base of the cloud,

56
00:04:16,841 --> 00:04:18,880
they collide with liquid water drops,

57
00:04:18,881 --> 00:04:20,640
and then they collect them as well.

58
00:04:20,641 --> 00:04:23,020
Now, with the updraft being tilted and strong,

59
00:04:23,021 --> 00:04:25,120
as that particle begins to fall down,

60
00:04:25,121 --> 00:04:27,180
it can fall back into the updraft.

61
00:04:27,181 --> 00:04:28,860
And that allows the updraft, if it's strong,

62
00:04:28,861 --> 00:04:30,760
to take it back up to the top again.

63
00:04:30,761 --> 00:04:34,360
And so hailstorms form as they get cycled through a cloud

64
00:04:34,361 --> 00:04:36,800
three, four, or maybe even five times

65
00:04:36,801 --> 00:04:38,160
for the really large ones.

66
00:04:38,161 --> 00:04:39,900
And at some point, they get so large

67
00:04:39,901 --> 00:04:41,740
that even the updraft can't keep them in there,

68
00:04:41,741 --> 00:04:43,640
and they fall out the base of the cloud

69
00:04:43,641 --> 00:04:44,540
as hell.

70
00:04:47,580 --> 00:04:50,680
Hail comes in all sizes.

71
00:04:50,780 --> 00:04:52,440
And the official terms designated

72
00:04:52,540 --> 00:04:55,420
by the National Weather Service are as straightforward

73
00:04:55,520 --> 00:04:56,580
as they are colorful.

74
00:04:59,220 --> 00:05:05,120
The smallest size is pea, followed by marble, dime, quarter,

75
00:05:05,220 --> 00:05:09,140
golf ball, tennis ball, baseball, and grapefruit.

76
00:05:13,640 --> 00:05:17,640
The largest hailstone on record fell in Aurora, Nebraska, in

77
00:05:17,740 --> 00:05:20,240
June 2003.

78
00:05:20,340 --> 00:05:24,580
Its diameter was a whopping 6.5 inches, its circumference

79
00:05:24,680 --> 00:05:26,680
17.37 inches.

80
00:05:34,200 --> 00:05:37,440
Aurora's record hailstone fell within a thunderstorm-prone

81
00:05:37,540 --> 00:05:40,740
region as one of the Earth's most heavily targeted hail

82
00:05:40,840 --> 00:05:42,340
belts.

83
00:05:42,341 --> 00:05:48,140
Since 1980, four states, Texas, Oklahoma, Kansas, and Nebraska

84
00:05:48,141 --> 00:05:52,380
have accounted for 42% of the 3,000 hailstorms America

85
00:05:52,381 --> 00:05:53,980
averages each year.

86
00:05:57,580 --> 00:06:01,620
Hail has killed only a handful of people in the United States

87
00:06:01,621 --> 00:06:04,860
and lacks the sheer devastation associated with tornadoes and

88
00:06:04,861 --> 00:06:06,700
hurricanes.

89
00:06:06,701 --> 00:06:11,460
But in terms of property damage, few natural phenomena come

90
00:06:11,461 --> 00:06:16,760
phenomena come close. Every year, hail produces about

91
00:06:16,761 --> 00:06:21,460
a billion dollars of losses for the insurance industry as a whole.

92
00:06:21,461 --> 00:06:25,900
Of the top ten biggest catastrophes that State Farm has

93
00:06:25,901 --> 00:06:29,320
experienced, nearly half of them are from hail losses.

94
00:06:33,320 --> 00:06:36,620
Laird McDonald conducts hail impact tests at State Farm

95
00:06:36,621 --> 00:06:41,220
Insurance's lab in Bloomington, Illinois.

96
00:06:41,221 --> 00:06:43,720
Three, two, one.

97
00:06:47,720 --> 00:06:50,420
Different materials react differently

98
00:06:50,421 --> 00:06:53,960
to different types of applied forces.

99
00:06:53,961 --> 00:06:57,760
We want to know how likely it is those materials will be

100
00:06:57,761 --> 00:07:00,980
damaged and result in a loss for State Farm

101
00:07:00,981 --> 00:07:04,580
under different types of conditions.

102
00:07:04,581 --> 00:07:08,280
State Farm's research team creates artificial hailstones

103
00:07:08,281 --> 00:07:13,280
water in aluminum molds. It makes them as large as 2 and 1

104
00:07:13,380 --> 00:07:16,280
quarter inches in diameter, the size of tennis balls.

105
00:07:19,720 --> 00:07:22,800
The ice balls are fired from the specially designed compressed

106
00:07:22,900 --> 00:07:27,960
air hail gun. It can fire the balls at speeds

107
00:07:28,060 --> 00:07:33,700
up to 90 miles per hour, replicating the maximum impact

108
00:07:33,701 --> 00:07:37,200
impact energy is seen in all but the most extreme hailstorms.

109
00:07:43,640 --> 00:07:47,540
A frequent test sample is a section of roof.

110
00:07:47,640 --> 00:07:51,060
Since roofs cover a significant portion of the urban landscape,

111
00:07:51,160 --> 00:07:53,060
they're one of hail's most vulnerable targets.

112
00:07:57,120 --> 00:08:01,360
This is a typical S-type concrete tile,

113
00:08:01,361 --> 00:08:05,360
And we will typically impact at the top of the curve,

114
00:08:05,460 --> 00:08:09,400
the inside curve, and one of the corners,

115
00:08:09,500 --> 00:08:13,000
since these are areas of typical vulnerability

116
00:08:13,100 --> 00:08:14,300
with this type of product.

117
00:08:18,420 --> 00:08:21,520
Multiple impacts with inch and three-quarter ice balls

118
00:08:21,620 --> 00:08:23,220
simulate a severe hailstorm.

119
00:08:23,221 --> 00:08:30,560
storm. The concrete tile shows no sign

120
00:08:30,561 --> 00:08:33,360
of weakness until impact number five.

121
00:08:36,660 --> 00:08:41,680
A hairline crack appears. Yet another impact, with an ice

122
00:08:41,681 --> 00:08:45,080
ball two and a quarter inches in diameter, widens it.

123
00:08:49,180 --> 00:08:52,380
The weakened tile awaits impact number seven.

124
00:08:52,381 --> 00:08:54,780
in 3, 2, 1.

125
00:09:01,580 --> 00:09:04,660
Ice in the sky presents an even more deadly threat

126
00:09:04,661 --> 00:09:06,260
when we venture up to meet it in the clouds.

127
00:09:10,060 --> 00:09:15,240
Since 1993, ice has brought down 135 small and medium-sized

128
00:09:15,241 --> 00:09:20,640
aircraft and killed 171 people.

129
00:09:20,641 --> 00:09:24,120
Icing occurs generally at altitudes no higher than 23,000

130
00:09:24,121 --> 00:09:27,580
feet. Above that level, the air is so

131
00:09:27,581 --> 00:09:31,080
cold that water vapor has solidified into ice, which

132
00:09:31,081 --> 00:09:34,860
bounces off aircraft. But below that threshold,

133
00:09:35,280 --> 00:09:38,220
clouds can be littered with so-called supercooled liquid

134
00:09:38,221 --> 00:09:42,020
water droplets. They remain liquid even below

135
00:09:42,021 --> 00:09:45,040
water's freezing point because they have no dust particle or

136
00:09:45,041 --> 00:09:47,760
other nuclei around which they can crystallize.

137
00:09:47,761 --> 00:09:51,460
Now, the danger of that is, if you're driving a plane

138
00:09:51,560 --> 00:09:54,560
through a cloud and it interacts with these supercooled

139
00:09:54,660 --> 00:09:57,000
droplets, as soon as they come into contact with something

140
00:09:57,100 --> 00:10:00,700
that's below freezing, they immediately freeze.

141
00:10:00,800 --> 00:10:03,000
So you get ice on the wings of the aircraft,

142
00:10:03,100 --> 00:10:05,620
and that's not good.

143
00:10:05,720 --> 00:10:07,920
A common misconception persists

144
00:10:08,020 --> 00:10:12,520
that the ice's added weight causes aircraft to crash.

145
00:10:12,620 --> 00:10:16,360
But ice imperils planes by deforming the shape,

146
00:10:16,361 --> 00:10:20,360
and the aerodynamics of their wings.

147
00:10:20,460 --> 00:10:24,000
Ice as thin as coarse sandpaper on the leading edge or upper

148
00:10:24,100 --> 00:10:28,500
surface can disrupt the airflow around the wing, reducing its

149
00:10:28,600 --> 00:10:30,800
lift and increasing its drag.

150
00:10:33,420 --> 00:10:35,480
Insufficient lift could force the plane

151
00:10:35,580 --> 00:10:38,020
into an unrecoverable dive.

152
00:10:38,120 --> 00:10:41,460
Too much drag could slow the plane enough to force its nose

153
00:10:41,560 --> 00:10:45,460
up, causing a stall, sending the aircraft plunging

154
00:10:45,461 --> 00:10:54,700
plunging toward the ground. Since 1944, engineers at NASA's

155
00:10:54,701 --> 00:10:57,700
Icing Research Tunnel in Cleveland, Ohio, have been

156
00:10:57,701 --> 00:11:01,100
studying the dynamics of icing to develop ways to combat it.

157
00:11:01,101 --> 00:11:03,720
It's a simulation of the natural environment, but we

158
00:11:03,721 --> 00:11:06,320
have control over the temperature, the amount of

159
00:11:06,321 --> 00:11:09,320
liquid water, the drop size, everything that you want to

160
00:11:09,321 --> 00:11:12,120
have control over, which we don't have control over in

161
00:11:12,121 --> 00:11:17,120
nature we can do here in the tunnel.

162
00:11:17,220 --> 00:11:20,460
The tunnel is a closed loop that recirculates its airflow

163
00:11:20,560 --> 00:11:24,260
through four turning vanes positioned at each corner.

164
00:11:24,360 --> 00:11:28,360
A 25-foot fan generates its wind stream.

165
00:11:28,460 --> 00:11:31,280
Powered by a 5,000 horsepower engine,

166
00:11:31,380 --> 00:11:34,480
the fan can produce air speeds up to 400 miles per hour.

167
00:11:38,120 --> 00:11:41,380
The wind it creates passes through an 1,800-ton heat

168
00:11:41,381 --> 00:11:44,820
heat exchanger to duplicate the frigid temperatures at icing

169
00:11:44,821 --> 00:11:49,140
altitudes. This massive air conditioner,

170
00:11:49,141 --> 00:11:52,500
in which refrigerant pumps through a network of coils,

171
00:11:52,501 --> 00:11:54,980
can plunge the tunnel's temperature as low as 25

172
00:11:54,981 --> 00:12:02,220
degrees below zero Fahrenheit. The freezing air then speeds

173
00:12:02,221 --> 00:12:05,300
through a grid of spray nozzles that functions as the tunnel's

174
00:12:05,301 --> 00:12:11,300
cloud. 250 nozzles like this one,

175
00:12:11,301 --> 00:12:14,300
eject the tunnel's supercooled water droplets.

176
00:12:14,301 --> 00:12:17,060
And by controlling the air pressure and water pressure,

177
00:12:17,061 --> 00:12:21,420
we can control the drop size from 15 microns to 50 microns.

178
00:12:21,421 --> 00:12:26,140
One micron is .00004 of an inch.

179
00:12:26,141 --> 00:12:30,480
So average droplet size, 20 microns,

180
00:12:30,481 --> 00:12:33,540
it's kind of like equivalent of pollen floating around

181
00:12:33,541 --> 00:12:34,241
in the air.

182
00:12:34,242 --> 00:12:35,940
That's how small the water droplets are.

183
00:12:35,941 --> 00:12:42,940
Past the spray bars lies the tunnel's test section.

184
00:12:42,941 --> 00:12:49,940
It measures 20 feet long, 9 feet wide, and 6 feet high.

185
00:12:49,941 --> 00:12:52,940
The full-scale aircraft components slated to be tested

186
00:12:52,941 --> 00:12:55,940
here lower by crane into position.

187
00:12:58,940 --> 00:13:02,940
Engineers are preparing to study icing's effects on this wing

188
00:13:02,941 --> 00:13:04,100
on this wing segment.

189
00:13:06,040 --> 00:13:07,180
Okay, ready to go?

190
00:13:08,620 --> 00:13:11,320
Okay, you wanna give us a countdown for spray-on?

191
00:13:12,940 --> 00:13:17,920
Four, three, two, one, spray-on.

192
00:13:22,400 --> 00:13:27,460
Okay, we're at negative 9.5 C at 174 miles an hour, Dave.

193
00:13:27,461 --> 00:13:30,000
Okay, we'll give it a little bit more time

194
00:13:30,001 --> 00:13:31,900
with the, for the model for temperatures.

195
00:13:32,940 --> 00:13:35,980
During the 10-minute test, the supercooled water droplets

196
00:13:36,080 --> 00:13:38,840
freeze almost instantly as they strike the surface

197
00:13:38,940 --> 00:13:40,040
of the wing's leading edge.

198
00:13:43,960 --> 00:13:46,120
A subsequent examination of the ice buildup

199
00:13:46,220 --> 00:13:48,420
reveals the kind of jagged protrusions

200
00:13:48,520 --> 00:13:50,700
that could disrupt the wing's aerodynamics

201
00:13:50,800 --> 00:13:52,060
and trigger a crash.

202
00:13:52,160 --> 00:13:55,760
OK, we can see we had a nice glazed ice condition.

203
00:13:55,860 --> 00:13:58,500
Tests like these have helped engineers devise a number

204
00:13:58,600 --> 00:13:59,640
of icing countermeasures.

205
00:13:59,641 --> 00:14:04,880
At cold weather airports, workers remove ice from planes

206
00:14:04,980 --> 00:14:07,880
by spraying fluids like propylene glycol,

207
00:14:07,980 --> 00:14:11,380
similar to anti-freeze used in cars.

208
00:14:11,480 --> 00:14:14,980
It can reduce the freezing point of moisture on the wing surface

209
00:14:15,080 --> 00:14:19,900
to 60 degrees below zero Fahrenheit.

210
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:22,120
And large commercial jets counter ice

211
00:14:22,220 --> 00:14:24,340
by rooting hot air from the engines

212
00:14:24,440 --> 00:14:27,140
through the leading edges of their wings.

213
00:14:27,141 --> 00:14:29,640
The heat prevents any super-cooled water droplets

214
00:14:29,641 --> 00:14:31,640
that strike the leading edge from freezing.

215
00:14:34,640 --> 00:14:38,180
Let that be a comfort to you on your next flight as you pass

216
00:14:38,181 --> 00:14:40,180
through a frigid cloud at 20,000 feet.

217
00:14:43,680 --> 00:14:47,600
As ice in the sky compels us to counter its persistent assault,

218
00:14:47,601 --> 00:14:51,100
the slippery ice right beneath our feet enables us to reach

219
00:14:51,101 --> 00:14:52,600
breathtaking speeds.

220
00:14:57,140 --> 00:15:02,140
One of our closest and favorite encounters with ice happens on

221
00:15:02,240 --> 00:15:07,140
a sleek slab 200 feet long and 85 feet wide.

222
00:15:07,240 --> 00:15:13,140
For America's 100 million hockey fans and 7 million diehard

223
00:15:13,240 --> 00:15:18,140
skaters, nothing is cooler, literally, than the ice rink.

224
00:15:22,140 --> 00:15:26,140
Few, however, give much thought to how the experts craft its

225
00:15:26,141 --> 00:15:30,640
its pristine, glistening surface.

226
00:15:30,740 --> 00:15:33,940
It all starts with a concrete slab embedded with refrigeration

227
00:15:34,040 --> 00:15:39,480
pipes that cool the concrete to 15 degrees Fahrenheit.

228
00:15:39,580 --> 00:15:43,300
Zamboni machines apply a thin layer of water to the slab,

229
00:15:43,400 --> 00:15:45,900
water that's treated to remove minerals that would otherwise

230
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:49,400
make the ice too soft and inconsistent.

231
00:15:49,500 --> 00:15:53,440
After it freezes, workers with hoses apply more water.

232
00:15:53,441 --> 00:15:56,840
After it, too, freezes, they lay down multiple coats of white

233
00:15:56,841 --> 00:16:01,140
paint. Without it, the rink's ice would end up looking as gray

234
00:16:01,141 --> 00:16:06,580
as its concrete base. Next, it's time to add the lines

235
00:16:06,581 --> 00:16:10,600
and markings, in this case, for a hockey surface.

236
00:16:14,600 --> 00:16:17,900
Finally, workers spray on additional layers of water, which

237
00:16:17,901 --> 00:16:22,500
freeze to seal the painted layers beneath it.

238
00:16:23,440 --> 00:16:27,880
Whether a rink is designed for hockey or speed skating,

239
00:16:27,980 --> 00:16:31,680
the ongoing challenge is keeping the surface cold, hard,

240
00:16:31,780 --> 00:16:35,880
and above all, fast.

241
00:16:42,900 --> 00:16:47,300
One world-class rink, the Utah Olympic Oval in Salt Lake City,

242
00:16:47,400 --> 00:16:49,900
stakes a claim as the fastest ice on Earth.

243
00:16:49,901 --> 00:16:54,940
on Earth. It was the venue for the speed skating

244
00:16:54,941 --> 00:16:59,040
events at the 2002 Winter Olympiad, and a slew of world

245
00:16:59,041 --> 00:17:01,140
records set here have yet to be broken.

246
00:17:05,140 --> 00:17:09,880
A massive refrigeration plant keeps the ice frozen.

247
00:17:09,881 --> 00:17:13,860
It uses ammonia to cool 1,000 tons of saltwater brine to

248
00:17:13,861 --> 00:17:17,760
between 15 and 18 degrees Fahrenheit.

249
00:17:17,761 --> 00:17:20,520
The brine remains liquid below water's freezing point

250
00:17:20,521 --> 00:17:23,400
because its salt interferes with the formation of ice crystals.

251
00:17:27,480 --> 00:17:30,820
Beneath the 90,000 square foot surface of the ice,

252
00:17:30,821 --> 00:17:34,840
the chilled brine flows through 33 miles of pipe.

253
00:17:34,841 --> 00:17:37,920
And these green pipes, we're bringing brine through.

